Title:
Reversing a Signed Driver (winhelper.sys) Packed with VMProtect3

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post walks through reversing a suspicious, signed Windows kernel driver (“winhelper.sys”) dropped by a VMProtect3-packed loader tied to a pay-to-cheat service.  
- The driver is unusually large (~2MB), signed with a revoked/expired EV certificate, and has a misleading 2015 timestamp, then is itself VMProtect3-virtualized.  
- After devirtualization, the author locates the driver’s IRP dispatch handler and analyzes its IOCTL interface, including an XOR/bitwise “encrypted” control code scheme.  
- The core functionality is a kernel-assisted process memory toolkit: get process base address, read process memory, and write process memory.  
- Reads/writes are implemented via virtual-to-physical translation and kernel primitives like MmCopyMemory and MmMapIoSpaceEx, enabling stealthier memory access patterns.  
- It’s useful for reverse engineers, malware/cheat analysts, and defenders investigating abused signed drivers and kernel memory manipulation techniques.

Technical Focus:
- VMProtect3 packing/virtualization and devirtualization workflow
- Windows kernel driver IRP dispatch / IOCTL interface reversing
- PsLookupProcessByProcessId, EPROCESS, PsGetProcessSectionBaseAddress
- Virtual-to-physical address translation for memory operations
- MmCopyMemory (physical memory reads) and MmMapIoSpaceEx (mapped writes)
- Abusing revoked/expired code-signing (mention of HookSigntool)

Use Cases:
- Reverse engineering packed/virtualized kernel drivers used by cheats/malware
- Building detections for suspicious IOCTL patterns and kernel memory access
- Incident response triage of dropped .sys drivers in System32
- Understanding kernel-assisted usermode memory read/write implementations

Keywords:
Windows kernel driver, winhelper.sys, VMProtect3, devirtualization, IRP dispatch, IOCTL, DeviceIoControl, EPROCESS, PsLookupProcessByProcessId, PsGetProcessSectionBaseAddress, MmCopyMemory, MmMapIoSpaceEx, physical memory, virtual-to-physical translation, kernel read/write, revoked certificate, EV code signing, HookSigntool, cheat driver